<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439060_4.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439060_4.jpg"></a>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_1.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_1.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439283_1.jpg"></a>
<p>
Driving along to the next destination, the world's largest ginger factory in Yalinda, we took the scenic ocean route to see more of the beaches.  M had been having this craving for ginger for some time today and was quite excited to get there.  As we finally pulled into the parking lot, M's wishes would be answered.  We took the tour that showed us the processing plant and gave us some sample tastings.  It's amazing how ginger is processed.  The amount of time the ginger sits in the ground, the more spicier it becomes.  After it is harvested and peeled, it is stirred for many day until the syrup seperates from it.  That syrup is commonly used in ginger beer.  The rest of the ginger is used in foods.  Australia is one of the largest exporters of ginger in the world.  M ended up getting some ginger candy we can use on the trip to chew on.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_2.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_2.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439283_2.jpg"></a>
<p>
More ocean driving now taking Hwy 1 down to the boardwalk at Caloundra to look at the rocky beaches.  This time I walked along the beach for awhile checking out the seashells I found.  The rocks had a green algae-like substance growing on it that liked pretty interesting as the sun was starting to set.
<p>
<!--
<img src=http://www.mybigadventure.com/ankesh/picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_3.jpg>-->
<a href="index.php?action=Pics&name=../picupload/Uploads/Images/1126439283_3.jpg"><img src="picupload/Uploads/Images/Small/1126439283_3.jpg"></a>
<p>
With just a little more time left we drove out to the Glasshouse Mountains.  They are oddly shaped mountains, some coming to a point.  Captain Cook had named them the glasshouse mountains because they had reminded him of glass furnaces, but I saw no resemblence whatsoever.
<p>
Having completed our goal for today, we drove two hours south to the city of Surfer's Paradise on the Gold Coast.  The hostel we stayed at this time was one of the best.  It was an old resort located right in the heart of the city.  Surfer's Paradise very much reminded me of Orlando,Florida meets Las Vegas.  It was a total party town with lots of bars and young kids lined up to go in them are drinking their brains out.  The town had it's own version of the strip called the Gold Coast Hwy.  And a very trendy shopping area called Cavill St. But, as I was saying, our accommodations were first class compared to the other places we had been staying and the price was unbeatable. 
